1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,140
Here is one of the loveliest
ideas in trigonometry:

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:06,420
a point going round a circle,
casting its height onto a wave.

3
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:07,600
Count the moving parts.

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,560
The radius. The dot on the circle.

5
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,640
The dashed link. The dot on the wave.

6
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,980
And the wave itself, growing behind it.

7
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:16,560
Five things, all moving in perfect sync.

8
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:19,820
You could try to animate each one
separately and hope they line up.

9
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,060
But there is a far better way,

10
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:22,900
and it's the subject of this episode.

11
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:27,553
Every one of those five parts
is driven by a single number.

12
00:00:27,583 --> 00:00:28,743
Let's start small.

13
00:00:28,743 --> 00:00:30,703
A dot, and a label above it.

14
00:00:30,703 --> 00:00:32,983
Move the dot, and the label stays behind,

15
00:00:32,983 --> 00:00:34,583
because nobody told it to move.

16
00:00:34,583 --> 00:00:36,143
We could animate the label too,

17
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:37,283
with the same shift.

18
00:00:37,283 --> 00:00:39,223
That works, for this one motion.

19
00:00:39,223 --> 00:00:40,943
But now every time the dot moves,

20
00:00:40,943 --> 00:00:43,103
you have to remember to
move the label as well,

21
00:00:43,103 --> 00:00:44,783
and work out exactly how.

22
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:46,543
If the dot travels along an arc,

23
00:00:46,543 --> 00:00:48,683
a straight shift for
the label won't keep up.

24
00:00:48,683 --> 00:00:51,463
What we really want to say is
not "move the label to here".

25
00:00:51,463 --> 00:00:52,303
It is a relationship.

26
00:00:52,303 --> 00:00:54,863
"The label is always just above the dot."

27
00:00:54,863 --> 00:00:58,450
And Manim has a way to say exactly that.

28
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:01,810
An updater is a function
that you attach to a mobject.

29
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:04,930
Then, every single frame,
before the frame is captured,

30
00:01:04,930 --> 00:01:06,450
Manim calls it.

31
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:08,190
Here, the updater says:

32
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:10,630
put m next to the dot, above it.

33
00:01:10,630 --> 00:01:13,830
And m is whatever object
the updater is attached to,

34
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:15,430
in this case the label.

35
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:20,150
The word lambda is just Python's way of
writing a small function in one line:

36
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:22,650
it takes m, and does this to it.

37
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:24,550
Now we only animate the dot.

38
00:01:24,550 --> 00:01:26,170
It moves right, the label follows.

39
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:28,570
It moves up, the label follows.

40
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:30,570
It swings around a full circle,

41
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:33,990
and every frame the updater
puts the label back above it.

42
00:01:33,990 --> 00:01:37,030
We never mention the label
in those animations at all.

43
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:38,990
When you want the relationship to end,

44
00:01:38,990 --> 00:01:40,530
call clear updaters.

45
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:43,890
The label stops following, and
the next time the dot moves,

46
00:01:43,890 --> 00:01:45,510
the label stays put.

47
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:48,450
This is a shift in how
you think about a scene.

48
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:50,810
Instead of scripting
every object's motion,

49
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:54,850
you script a few, and describe
how the rest relate to them.

50
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:56,730
Some things should change by themselves,

51
00:01:56,730 --> 00:01:59,990
as time passes. A spinning wheel.

52
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:04,190
A ticking clock. For those, an
updater can ask for a second argument,

53
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:07,470
d t, the amount of time
since the last frame.

54
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:12,230
Here, each wheel rotates by its
speed times d t every frame.

55
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:15,050
So the slow one turns a
quarter turn per second,

56
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:17,770
and the fast one half a turn per second.

57
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:20,710
And these wheels keep turning
even during a plain wait,

58
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:23,110
when no animation is playing at all.

59
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:28,310
Why multiply by d t, instead of just
rotating a fixed amount each frame?

60
00:02:28,310 --> 00:02:29,590
Because frame rates differ.

61
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:33,530
ManimOnline previews at
fifteen frames per second.

62
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:35,810
A finished video might use sixty.

63
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:36,710
With d t,

64
00:02:36,710 --> 00:02:40,370
each frame turns the wheel by the right
amount for the time that has actually

65
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:45,850
passed, and after one second, both
versions have turned exactly as far.

66
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:48,190
Your scene describes motion in seconds,

67
00:02:48,190 --> 00:02:52,083
not in frames, and looks
the same at any frame rate.

68
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:53,583
Now the key ingredient.

69
00:02:53,583 --> 00:02:57,023
A value tracker is a mobject
that holds a single number,

70
00:02:57,023 --> 00:02:59,083
and is never drawn.

71
00:02:59,083 --> 00:03:02,983
That sounds useless, until you
realise that because it is a mobject,

72
00:03:02,983 --> 00:03:05,843
you can animate it like anything else.

73
00:03:05,843 --> 00:03:11,383
x dot animate dot set value seven point
five smoothly changes the number from

74
00:03:11,383 --> 00:03:14,383
zero to seven point
five over three seconds,

75
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:17,463
using every idea from episode three:

76
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:19,543
alpha, interpolation and rate functions.

77
00:03:19,543 --> 00:03:23,143
Then other objects read the
number in their updaters.

78
00:03:23,143 --> 00:03:26,703
The pointer asks the number line
where seven point five lives,

79
00:03:26,703 --> 00:03:29,723
using n to p, short for number to point,

80
00:03:29,723 --> 00:03:31,343
and sits just above it.

81
00:03:31,343 --> 00:03:35,743
The decimal number readout sets its
displayed value to the tracker's value every

82
00:03:35,743 --> 00:03:38,483
frame, and parks itself above the pointer.

83
00:03:38,483 --> 00:03:40,823
So one animation, of one invisible number,

84
00:03:40,823 --> 00:03:43,363
moves the pointer and
the readout together.

85
00:03:43,363 --> 00:03:45,523
That's the pattern
you'll use over and over:

86
00:03:45,523 --> 00:03:48,363
decide which numbers your
scene is really about,

87
00:03:48,363 --> 00:03:52,683
store them in value trackers,
and let everything else follow.

88
00:03:52,683 --> 00:03:56,003
Sometimes it's easier to rebuild
an object than to adjust it.

89
00:03:56,003 --> 00:03:58,083
This triangle has a corner on the circle,

90
00:03:58,083 --> 00:04:02,343
and its shape depends on the angle in a
way that no single shift or scale could

91
00:04:02,343 --> 00:04:03,243
describe.

92
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:07,623
Always redraw takes a function that
builds the object from scratch.

93
00:04:07,623 --> 00:04:12,543
Every frame, Manim calls that function
again and swaps in the brand new result.

94
00:04:12,543 --> 00:04:15,023
So the triangle is rebuilt
from the current angle,

95
00:04:15,023 --> 00:04:17,943
sixty times a second in a finished video,

96
00:04:17,943 --> 00:04:22,503
and the cosine and sine labels
are rebuilt beside its sides.

97
00:04:22,503 --> 00:04:25,263
It's the most flexible
tool in this episode,

98
00:04:25,263 --> 00:04:29,983
and also the most expensive, because
it creates new objects every frame.

99
00:04:29,983 --> 00:04:32,943
For a handful of shapes
that's no problem at all.

100
00:04:32,943 --> 00:04:35,863
If you find yourself
redrawing hundreds of objects,

101
00:04:35,863 --> 00:04:39,250
look for an updater that
adjusts them instead.

102
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:41,490
Now the opening scene makes sense.

103
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:43,590
There is one value tracker, t,

104
00:04:43,590 --> 00:04:47,350
the angle. Two little functions
turn t into positions:

105
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,870
one on the circle, one on the wave.

106
00:04:49,870 --> 00:04:54,610
The radius, the dashed link and both
dots are always redrawn from those

107
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:55,450
positions.

108
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:57,270
The wave is a traced path.

109
00:04:57,270 --> 00:04:59,970
It's given a function that
says where the wave dot is,

110
00:04:59,970 --> 00:05:03,730
and every frame it adds that
point to a growing curve.

111
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:06,590
So the wave isn't calculated in advance.

112
00:05:06,590 --> 00:05:08,790
It's literally drawn by the moving point.

113
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:11,790
And the whole scene is driven by one line:

114
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:14,570
animate t from zero to
two pi over six seconds,

115
00:05:14,570 --> 00:05:16,550
at a steady linear rate.

116
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:19,950
One number changes, and five things move.

117
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:22,270
Two things tend to surprise people.

118
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:25,450
First: updaters keep running
while an animation plays.

119
00:05:25,450 --> 00:05:29,870
Manim even applies them to the
animation's starting and ending copies,

120
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:33,670
so a spinning wheel keeps spinning
while it glides across the screen.

121
00:05:33,670 --> 00:05:36,370
But if an updater decides
where an object is,

122
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:38,510
animating its position does nothing:

123
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:41,150
the updater puts it back, every frame.

124
00:05:41,150 --> 00:05:45,750
Animating its colour works, because
the updater never touches colour.

125
00:05:45,750 --> 00:05:47,850
To move a follower, move what it follows,

126
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:50,250
or clear its updater first.

127
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:52,370
Second: updaters run in order.

128
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:56,350
If one object's updater reads another
object that hasn't been updated yet this

129
00:05:56,350 --> 00:05:58,610
frame, it sees last frame's position,

130
00:05:58,610 --> 00:06:00,730
and lags one frame behind.

131
00:06:00,730 --> 00:06:02,190
Usually that's invisible.

132
00:06:02,190 --> 00:06:05,450
When it isn't, have both read
the same value tracker directly,

133
00:06:05,450 --> 00:06:07,900
rather than reading each other.

134
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:11,320
With this many tools, it
helps to have a way to choose.

135
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,440
If an object should follow another,

136
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:17,060
attach an updater that places
it relative to the leader.

137
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:19,660
If it should change steadily by itself,

138
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:23,340
like a spinning wheel,
use an updater with d t.

139
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:26,080
If several objects depend
on the same quantity,

140
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:28,360
put that quantity in a value tracker,

141
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,380
and let each object read it.

142
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:33,040
If an object's very shape
depends on that quantity,

143
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:36,640
so that it's easier to
rebuild than to adjust,

144
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,620
use always redraw.

145
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:40,980
And if you want the
history of a moving point,

146
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:42,500
use a traced path.

147
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:45,680
It's worth knowing that
always redraw isn't magic.

148
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:49,100
It's an updater that
calls become every frame:

149
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:53,120
become makes an object take on the
shape and style of another one,

150
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:54,740
while staying the same object,

151
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:58,140
so it stays in the scene
and keeps its name.

152
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:02,080
You can write that updater yourself
whenever you need a variation.

153
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:04,490
And finally, the most important option:

154
00:07:04,490 --> 00:07:08,340
if something changes once, at
a moment you know in advance,

155
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,420
don't use an updater at all.

156
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:14,480
A plain animation is simpler
to read and easier to change.

157
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:18,816
Updaters are for relationships
that must hold all the time.

158
00:07:18,816 --> 00:07:22,096
Updaters describe relationships
that hold every frame.

159
00:07:22,096 --> 00:07:24,656
With d t, they describe change over time,

160
00:07:24,656 --> 00:07:26,596
independent of frame rate.

161
00:07:26,596 --> 00:07:29,776
Value trackers hold the numbers
that a scene is really about,

162
00:07:29,776 --> 00:07:32,356
so animating one number
can move everything.

163
00:07:32,356 --> 00:07:36,396
Always redraw rebuilds objects whose
shape depends on those numbers.

164
00:07:36,396 --> 00:07:39,516
And traced paths draw the
history of a moving point.

165
00:07:39,516 --> 00:07:43,236
Next time, we bring all of this
to the place it shines brightest:

166
00:07:43,236 --> 00:07:46,751
graphs, areas under curves, and
the slope of a tangent line.
